Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Inhale Opens the White Oscillator
- Story & Context: Mali begins with a two-register khaen cluster sustained through alternating breath. Elise assigns a pale square-wave synth to inhalation and a darker sawtooth stab to exhalation, but both play the same pitch set. The listener therefore hears timbre flip while harmony remains fixed. Kiet enters one beat after each fingering change with a clipped two-note electric-piano answer. Arun remains silent for the first sixteen bars. When Mali sustains the same breath direction but changes one upper pipe, the first dry handclap appears, establishing the Mirror Clap rule. In the final section, Elise removes all synths and leaves only drum machine, khaen, and the delayed electric-piano replicas; the supposedly electronic architecture is revealed to have been encoded in the acoustic performance from the start.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Darkroom scene with a vertical khaen reflected in two stainless processing trays, one lit white and one deep red, compact synthesizer LEDs mirrored on wet tile, prepared electric-piano keys in shallow focus. No full sung lyrics; sparse spoken cues limited to “inhale: white,” “exhale: black,” “same pipes,” and “change one finger.”

Track 2: Contact Sheet With One Missing Clap
- Story & Context: Elise programs a rigid four-on-the-floor-adjacent electroclash pattern but removes one expected clap every eight bars. Mali plays a repeating khaen phrase that continues through the absence without emphasizing it. Kiet treats the missing clap as space for a damped electric-piano knock, but only on alternate cycles, so the listener never knows whether the gap will remain empty. Arun strikes a steel tray after every fourth omitted clap, letting one metallic resonance cross into the next bar. Midway through, Mali changes breath direction earlier than expected; Elise's synth family flips before the drum phrase ends, making the timbral boundary and barline disagree. The track closes with the drum machine maintaining the grid while the khaen sustains across two complete measures without changing breath.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Photographic contact sheet pinned above a stainless darkroom sink, every eighth frame blank, a handclap symbol crossed out in grease pencil, khaen pipes and prepared-piano tines reflected in wet metal. Spoken fragments limited to “leave frame eight blank,” “do not clap there,” “the breath crossed early,” and “keep printing.”

Track 3: Red Lamp Stays On After the Sequence Ends
- Story & Context: The EP closer begins at maximum electronic density by the band's standards: compact kick-snare grid, square-wave bass, two alternating synth stabs, and clipped prepared-piano dyads. Mali responds with the sparsest khaen material on the record, using only a few pipes at a time and allowing the natural reed chatter to remain exposed. Over several minutes, the arrangement subtracts rather than adds. Kiet removes every second response. Arun dampens the brake drum immediately after each strike. Elise shortens the bass notes until they become tiny voltage clicks. Finally the sequencer stops entirely while Mali continues one unbroken breath cycle. The red safety lamp and ventilation fan remain audible in the room microphones. The last event is not a note but the soft mechanical closure of one khaen finger hole after the electronics have ended.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty photo lab under a single red safety lamp, sequencer display dark, khaen player still standing beside stainless trays, prepared electric piano and brake drum abandoned in shadow, ventilation duct above. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “sequence ends first,” “red stays on,” “keep the air moving,” and “close one hole.”

